{
	title: 'A turn stops when it should: round cap, stop reasons, killed tools',
	spec: 'A turn never spirals and never leaves a dangerous command running after the user stopped it. (1) Round cap: a turn runs at most `maxRounds` provider rounds (a config.ason setting in src/common/settings.ts, default 200; not shown in the UI beyond the pause reason); reaching it pauses the turn with a reason saying so, and Enter continues for another maxRounds. (2) Stop reasons, as the old Hal (~/.hal/src/server/providers/anthropic.ts, NORMAL_STOP_REASONS): a round that ends because of max_tokens (or context window exceeded) or a refusal runs none of its tool calls (a call cut off mid-input is not a call) and ends the turn as an error that says why ("Response stopped: max_tokens", or the refusal\'s explanation); any other ending runs the tool calls it holds. (3) Stopping kills tools: Escape, and quitting the last Hal process (Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, SIGHUP: all a deliberate stop, so the turn pauses as tasks/j1/states.md says for Ctrl-C), kill the process tree of every running bash call: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 2 s (the old Hal\'s bash.ts killProcessTree, which reaches background jobs of the detached group). The killed call\'s result says it was stopped. Tests: a fake provider that never stops asking for tools pauses at the cap; max_tokens with a tool call runs nothing and errors; Escape during `sleep 30 & sleep 30` leaves no sleep alive.',
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['1r'],
	foldInto: ['sj', '1r', 'j1'],
	notes: [
		"Stop reasons stay neutral in the providers: parse yields done with reason max_tokens/refusal (DoneEvent.explanation carries Anthropic's stop_details.explanation); turns.stopped() turns that into the error, so fake providers in tests exercise it. A tool call whose JSON is broken is only an error when the stop reason is not max_tokens (anthropic defers it to message_stop; openai-compat skips it on finish_reason length).",
		"Complete tool calls of a refused or max_tokens round stay in history unanswered; replay already tells the model 'Tool call did not run: the turn error.'",
		"The round cap counts finished rounds per runTurn call, so continue, a question's answer and a recovery each start a fresh count. The pause reason goes in turn_end.pauseReason (history.end's third argument). Continuing after a pause between tool rounds does not tell the model why it was paused: replay only puts endNote in front of the next typed prompt.",
		"tools.killGroup SIGTERMs the group and has a detached `sh -c 'sleep 2; kill -9 -pgid'` send the SIGKILL, so it still arrives when Hal exits first. A setTimeout cannot: the exit handler (host.init) aborts every running turn when pauseOnExit, and timers never fire after exit. The SIGKILL is sent even if bash already closed: a background job with its output redirected keeps the group alive after the pipe closes.",
		'SIGTERM/SIGHUP go through terminal.quit(code) in realIO, so they call onQuit (pause if last) like Ctrl-C. A headless process (no terminal) has no such handlers: SIGTERM leaves its turns unfinished for the next host.',
		'Tests find leftover processes with pgrep -f on a unique sleep duration (sleep 30.<pid>1); bash children are detached, so the e2e afterEach group kill does not reach them.'
	]
}
